# What never enters the build context. The image needs
# src/distro/packs/zsh/config (the socle - the zsh pack's own folder) and
# src/distro/build/first_boot.sh (see the COPY lines in the Dockerfile);
# everything else is either noise sent to the Docker daemon or user
# configuration that must not be baked into /etc/skel.
#
# This file sits BESIDE the Dockerfile under the name Dockerfile.dockerignore
# - the only name a builder reads next to a `-f` Dockerfile (BuildKit) - but
# its patterns are written against the CONTEXT ROOT, the repository: they
# match ./src/windows and so on, never this folder.
#
# distro/packs/ is in this list because the image carries no pack: a pack
# arrives on the instance, from Windows. The one exception is the zsh pack's
# config/ - the socle the COPY line reads. A child cannot be re-included once
# its folder itself is excluded, so the folder is not: its children are, one
# level down, with the socle's path put back.
.git
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github
.claude
assets
docs
src/distro/packs/*
!src/distro/packs/zsh
src/distro/packs/zsh/*
!src/distro/packs/zsh/config
LICENSE
README.md
src/windows
wsl.ps1

# Build artifacts
*.tar
*.vhdx

# A local .env.global is one machine's configuration. The socle's COPY would
# bake it into /etc/skel, so every distro, for every user, would inherit it.
# `**/` earns its place: a pattern without a slash only matches at the root of
# the context, while the file this protects -
# src/distro/packs/zsh/config/gmake/.env.global - sits in a subdirectory. The
# two *.sample files are the committed contracts and they stay: `**/.env*`
# would have taken them along.
**/.env
**/.env.global
